From: OpenWrt MSM8916 Maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: pm8916: allow the RTC to be set

The PM8916 RTC has no battery on these sticks, so it comes out of reset at
1970-01-01 and the kernel seeds the system clock from it on every boot.  The
driver then only takes the "offset" path unless the node carries
allow-set-time, and that path needs the nvmem cell named "offset" -- which no
msm8916 board provides.  The result is that RTC_SET_TIME always fails:

    # hwclock -w
    hwclock: RTC_SET_TIME: No such device

so nothing can ever write the clock back.  The RTC therefore stays at 1970
across reboots, sysfixtime can only paper over it for the current boot, and
until NTP lands every TLS fetch fails ("certificate is not yet valid"), which
makes apk-key-refresh warn on every package operation.

Adding allow-set-time makes pm8xxx_rtc_set_time() program the RTC registers
directly, so the first boot with a plausible clock (from sysfixtime or NTP)
writes it back and every later boot starts sane.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8916.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8916.dtsi
@@ -173,6 +173,7 @@
 			reg = <0x6000>, <0x6100>;
 			reg-names = "rtc", "alarm";
 			interrupts = <0x0 0x61 0x1 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
+			allow-set-time;
 		};
 
 		pm8916_mpps: mpps@a000 {
